<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann II, Prince of Liechtenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Johann the Good</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann II, called the Good, was prince of Liechtenstein from 1858 to 1929, one of the longest reigns in European history. He granted the constitution of 1862, disbanded the army in 1868, and approved the constitution of 1921; a great art patron, he gave many works to museums.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-ii-prince-of-liechtenstein.jpg" alt="Johann II, Prince of Liechtenstein" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Clemens Kosel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_II_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the German Confederation dissolved, Prince <b>Johann II</b> disbanded the army in 1868, judging it pointless and too costly, and Liechtenstein has had no armed forces since.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Military</a></li></ul></section>
